--TEST--
a capability denial carries a `help:` line giving the grant line to add, the table it is written under and `nvs.toml`, and that table is the refused capability's own family — `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`, `rule:tooling/an-agent-asks-the-binary`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Whoever reads a refusal is about to write a grant, so the line under it is that
// grant: the folder of a refused file, or the refused host, and the block and file
// it goes in. The table is the family half of the capability's own name, which is
// why two refusals from two families point at two different blocks — and why the
// `[capabilities.fs]` this configuration already has is still the right place for `read`.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read("./data/note.txt");
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    var $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl("https://example.com/status");
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The file the help line names, holding a grant neither call needs: the tree
# resolves, and both refusals are about a capability that is missing rather
# than about a configuration that is.
[capabilities.fs]
write = ["./out"]
--FILE data/note.txt--
note
--EXPECTF--
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%edata%enote.txt, which is not granted
help: add `read = ["%sdata%s"]` under `[capabilities.fs]` in nvs.toml
Core\Http::allowUrl needs the capability `net.connect` for example.com, which is not granted
help: add `connect = ["example.com"]` under `[capabilities.net]` in nvs.toml
